WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.896
Parameterized queries are the core
prevention pattern.

2
00:00:02.976 --> 00:00:06.071
The idea is simple: the SQL command
structure is supplied by trusted code,

3
00:00:06.151 --> 00:00:09.844
while runtime values are passed separately
through placeholders or bound parameters.

4
00:00:09.924 --> 00:00:13.318
The driver and database then handle those
values as data rather than as part of the

5
00:00:13.398 --> 00:00:14.097
command syntax.

6
00:00:14.177 --> 00:00:16.773
In Go, teams commonly use the standard
database/sql package,

7
00:00:16.853 --> 00:00:20.047
driver-specific packages, prepared
statements, generated query layers,

8
00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:21.325
or higher-level libraries.

9
00:00:21.405 --> 00:00:24.200
The exact placeholder convention depends
on the database driver.

10
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.774
Some drivers use one marker style, while
others use numbered or named conventions.

11
00:00:27.854 --> 00:00:31.398
The important habit is to follow the
driver's documented convention and keep values

12
00:00:31.478 --> 00:00:34.373
in the argument list rather than adding
them directly to the SQL text.

13
00:00:34.453 --> 00:00:37.698
Bound parameters are appropriate for
values such as names, dates, statuses,

14
00:00:37.778 --> 00:00:41.022
account identifiers, tenant identifiers
stored as values, numeric limits,

15
00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:41.901
and filter values.

16
00:00:41.981 --> 00:00:45.475
Even when an input appears harmless,
parameterization should remain the default.

17
00:00:45.555 --> 00:00:48.949
Harmless-looking values can change over
time, be reused in a different feature,

18
00:00:49.029 --> 00:00:52.074
or come from a path that was not obvious
during the first implementation.

19
00:00:52.154 --> 00:00:53.901
Parameterization also improves review.

20
00:00:53.981 --> 00:00:57.325
A query with fixed structure and a clear
argument list is easier to inspect than a

21
00:00:57.405 --> 00:00:59.202
query assembled from many text fragments.

22
00:00:59.282 --> 00:01:02.027
Reviewers can focus on whether dynamic
structure is controlled,

23
00:01:02.107 --> 00:01:05.252
whether values are bound, and whether the
query matches the business rule.

24
00:01:05.332 --> 00:01:08.177
Prepared statements can help make repeated
query shapes explicit,

25
00:01:08.257 --> 00:01:11.751
but the security value comes from
preserving the separation between structure and

26
00:01:11.831 --> 00:01:12.180
values.
